From 1ab979444d83b66b03db0d28363097c32d3a9a4f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 01:37:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Blu-ray: a disc whose second view is not wanted is opened,
 listed and read as before

With the three commits that read the second view of a 3D Blu-ray, four things changed for
discs that have none, or on a display that does not take 3D:

- The player profile was no longer announced before a disc is opened. libbluray 1.5.0 sets the
  registers of a UHD player for a UHD disc whatever was announced (psr_init_UHD() is forced),
  so a UHD disc saw no difference; but a plain Blu-ray was left with the profile that the
  library starts with (profile 2, version 2.0) where it had profile 6, version 3.1, and a 3D
  disc became a 3D disc for everyone (profile 5 and the 3D registers).
- The playlists of a 3D disc were compared by their second view, so that of a 2D playlist and
  its 3D twin the 3D one was kept, and listed, chosen and played, also where only its base view
  can be shown.
- Every playlist that a disc plays was read a second time, by Kodi's own parser.
- A stream was demuxed without a parser if its item had a property named "noparse".

Now one answer, taken when a disc is opened (KODI::MULTIVIEW::DiscSecondViewWanted(): the
setting videoplayer.hades3dmvc, what the display says, the decoder h264_mvc, the playback
mode of stereoscopic videos), decides all of it:

- Not wanted: the profile is announced before the disc is opened, as always (the same value
  in the same register; it is now written after the three UHD registers instead of before
  them, and nothing reads any of them in between). Wanted: it is held back, and announced
  once the disc is open if the disc declares no 3D content. libbluray reads that register
  in between only for a disc that declares 3D content, and nothing of a disc plays before
  Open() returns: a plain Blu-ray and a UHD disc have the registers that they always had.
- The second view of a playlist is dropped from what CBlurayDirectory knows of it unless it
  is wanted. Every comparison added by the commit before then takes the path that it took
  (AreDifferentStereoscopicPresentations() is false, neither of two playlists is "3D", and
  IsRicherPresentation() goes on to the streams).
- The playlist is read again only for a disc that declares 3D content and whose second view
  is wanted, and only such a disc gets CDVDDemuxBluray3D. The playback mode "2D" is part of
  the one answer now, where the demuxer factory asked for it by itself.
- Only the stream that reads a clip of a disc (CDVDInputStreamBlurayFile) is demuxed without a
  parser.
- The root that a disc image is opened with stays what it was (empty); the udf:// form that
  Kodi's parser needs is kept apart.

The log says at LOGINFO what a disc that declares 3D content was taken for, and why its
second view is not read where it is not; for a disc whose second view is read, what each
playlist names, with the flag of the eye as Kodi and as libbluray read it.

A disc that declares no 3D content gets no new line and no new read.
